<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lu'an</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lu'an is a city in western Anhui province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, in the hills between the Huai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> rivers. Near it lay the ancient town of Lu, capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ying-bu">Ying Bu</a>'s Kingdom of Jiujiang after 206 BCE. The region is known for Lu'an Guapian, a celebrated green <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luan.jpg" alt="Lu'an" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: そらみみ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Main_hall_of_Tianfo_Temple_in_Dabieshan_Grottoes_Scenic_Area_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lu'an is a prefecture-level city in western Anhui province, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> to the northwest and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a> to the southwest. It lies between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and Huai rivers at the northern foot of the Dabie Mountains. The terrain is high in the southwest and flat in the northeast, forming three natural areas of mountains, hills and plains. The climate is humid subtropical with four distinct seasons, cold damp winters and hot humid summers, and most of the rain falls from June to August. The character 六, normally pronounced Liù, is read Lù here because of a historical literary reading.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the Xia and Shang dynasties Liu was a country south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a>. From the Western Zhou to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a> several feudal states, among them Ying, Liu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and Qunshu, arose in the area, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> they merged and became subordinate to Wu and then Chu. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> six counties belonged to Jiujiang County. The Western Han dynasty established the Liu'an Kingdom, taking the meaning &quot;safety in the six places&quot;, and the Eastern Han established Liu'an County. In 1118, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, the Lu'an Army was set up. Lu'an City and Lu'an County merged in December 1992, and the provincial-level Lu'an City was established in March 2000.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ying-bu">Ying Bu</a>, a general of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and early Han, came from the area. He had been one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-yu">Xiang Yu</a>'s generals and was granted the title of King of Jiujiang. He later surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bang">Liu Bang</a>, who named him King of Huainan, and he was killed for rebellion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lu'an Guapian, a green <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, is produced in the Dabie Mountains of Lu'an. It was called Luzhou Lu'an Tea in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> and appears in Lu Yu's Book of Tea. The Ming scientist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xu-guangqi">Xu Guangqi</a> wrote that the tea of Liu'an Prefecture was the best tea, and under the Qing it was listed as tribute. The leaf is shaped like a melon seed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-qirui">Duan Qirui</a>, a politician of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, was a native of Liu'an County. The local economy includes iron ore metallurgy, food processing and machinery manufacturing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lu'an” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
